    "reading": {
        "body": "AI companies are hiring heavily for legal, finance, and talent acquisition roles rather than AI research and engineering positions, signaling that operational scaling now constrains growth more than research capability.\n\nThe 18 openings split sharply between roles managing risk and resources—counsel for product and AI governance, finance specialists, operations managers—and talent acquisition itself. OpenAI alone has three active recruiter roles across geographies. This pattern reflects a mature constraint. Early-stage AI companies faced bottlenecks in research output and compute access. These postings suggest the constraint has shifted upstream: companies can acquire compute and build products, but cannot manage the legal exposure, financial complexity, and talent scarcity without dedicated infrastructure.\n\nThe concentration of legal hires is notable. Four of the 18 positions are counsel roles focused on AI product, data, and digital assets. This reflects genuine new risk—liability frameworks around AI systems remain unsettled, and companies selling or deploying AI face regulatory uncertainty that engineering alone cannot solve. Finance and operations hires cluster at Databricks and among newer entrants like Perplexity and Abridge, not only at incumbents.\n\nThe data does not capture how many positions go unfilled, whether these roles are new or replacements, or how compensation compares to research positions. It shows intent to hire, not hiring velocity or success. Geographic distribution spans San Francisco, London, Bengaluru, and the UAE, but the dataset size is too small to map where AI operations capacity is concentrating.",
